<section id="S5.SS3.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.3.2 </span>Effect of Pruning Criterion on Output Distribution</h4>

<div id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To quantitatively validate that our information entropy (IE) criterion better preserves the global prediction distribution than the cross-entropy
(CE) criterion, we analyzed the pruned Llama2-7B model over 5,000 prompts from the C4 dataset. The results, presented in Table <a href="#S5.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ 5.3.2 Effect of Pruning Criterion on Output Distribution ‣ 5.3 Ablation Study ‣ 5 Experiment ‣ High-Fidelity Pruning for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>,
confirm our method’s superior fidelity across two key metrics.
First, our IE criterion achieves a consistently lower Jensen-Shannon (JS) Distance. While
the improvement at 20% sparsity is modest, the advantage becomes more pronounced at the aggressive 30% ratio.
This trend highlights that our method’s ability to preserve the distribution’s overall shape is most critical in high-compression scenarios.
Second, the Top-15 Jaccard Similarity results provide complementary evidence, showing our approach better maintains the original
model’s most probable next tokens at both sparsity levels.
Qualitative examples that visually illustrate these findings are provided in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Qualitative Analysis: Distribution Visualization Examples ‣ High-Fidelity Pruning for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T7" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 7: </span>Comparison of pruning methods based on output distribution similarity.</figcaption>
